and  dollars  for a quick jaunt there is big money to  be made  that's one small step for man  somehow it all seems a little less noble  than in the early days when the apollo  astronauts left behind on the moon  a plaque reading we came in peace for  all mankind the new space tycoons may  not have the right  stuff but they've got all the bucks  and of course this is a brand new era in  space it's a commercialized space era  of course there are those who would  argue that that is in fact  the best way for peaceful uh uh  uh exploration and exploitation  of space and its resources as we  mentioned  on the 20th of july jeff bezos  the richest man on earth by some  measures will  go aloft in his blue origin spacecraft  into space  that is by coincidence or not the 50th  anniversary  of the apollo 11 landing on the moon  rob thank you very much for the moment  rob reynolds live  there from new mexico let's speak now to  leo enright who's a space analyst  and he's joining us from marseille in  the south of france very good to have  you with us a hotly anticipated space  flight today  how exciting is this launch today in the  wider history of space exploration do  you think the hype is justified  well the hype is interesting because  these two men both  bezos and branson are  children of the apollo era and there's  absolutely no question that both of  these  fabulously wealthy men have devoted  enormous amounts of money  uh to rekindling the apollo spirit  that was so alive 50 years ago so it has  potential  to get people fired up about access to  space  but of course as we've already  acknowledged several times  this is very much at the moment a rich  man's  a rich woman's club right when most  people think about  space flight they think about an  astronaut circling the earth floating in  space for at least a few days this  that's not going to be  what's happening today with with richard  branson is it talk us through  you know what's expected if everything  goes to plan